  "DETAIL_CN_ENG": {
   "null_rate": 0.0,
   "unique": 39,
   "top": {
    "\nYeongdogyo Bridge was originally called the ‘Wangsimpyeong Bridge’. In 1452, King Danjong, the 6th king of Joseon, became a king at the age of 11, and married Queen Jungsun two years later, who was a year older than him. They loved each other, but their happiness was disturbed by Prince Suyang, King Danjong's uncle. In 1455, King Danjong was dethroned by his uncle, and was exiled to Yeongwol, Gangwon-do Province. With tears, he parted with Queen Jungsun at the Yeongdogyo Bridge. Since then, people called this bridge ‘Yeongyibyeol’ or ‘Yeongyeong Gunnun’, meaning a lifelong separation. Later, King Sejo’s grandson, King Seongjong changed its name to ‘Yeongdogyo’, meaning a bridge that is eternally crossed by people.\nYeongdogyo Bridge was demolished during the period of King Gojong, the 26th king of Joseon, to be used as a construction material of Gyeongbokgung Palace. Afterwards, it was rebuilt by Japan in 1933 with concretes, but was destroyed once again during the covering construction of Cheonggyecheon Stream. Today’s Yeongdogyo Bridge was newly built during the restoration project of Cheonggyecheon Stream.\n\n ": 1,
    "\nt the mainstream of Cheonggyecheon, Sijeon Market and Jungchon Village were formed.\nThere were many translators and doctors living at the Jungchon Village. The official translators were wealthy, learned, and erudite on the topic of international circumstances, but they suffered from social discrimination because they were from middle-class families.\nIn the Late Joseon Dynasty, Banggakbon, the books that were printed by merchants for sale purposes, was widely circulated in the Sijeon Market. At the places where many people gathered, talk concerts were held by Jeongisu, who entertainingly read Banggakbon novels. In the late 18th century, the popularity of Jeongisu was tremendous. Wealthy families of Jungchon Village invited Jeongisu to their houses, and common people heard their stories at the streets. Jeongisu would keep their mouth closed right before the climax of a story, raising the curiosity of the listeners, and made them pay more money to hear more stories. Even the ladies of the gentry family invited Jeongisu. At that time, a male Jeongisu went to their house dressed up as a woman.\nThere were many pubs around Cheonggyecheon Stream, including the Jungchon Village. During the Joseon Dynasty, people who took the lead in this culture of entertainment were called ‘Walja’.": 1,
    "\nYun Seok-jung, a child literature writer, was born in Supyo-dong, Cheonggyecheon Stream in 1911 and spent his childhood there. He said, \"Cheonggyecheon is a clean stream, so women came out and washed clothes in the water. The stream was like a laundry, and women would gather there and chat with each other.\" Also, he stated that the child song “Pongdang Pongdang”, which is an onomatopoeia of the water’s splashing sound, is a song that he composed reminiscing his older sister who used to do the laundries at the stream. \nThis is very different from an old saying that excremental water flowed in the Cheonggyecheon Stream due to sewers in Hanyang. This is a misunderstanding. Since there were public wells in towns of Joseon, hygiene management was thorough in every village. During the period of Joseon Dynasty, relatively clean water flowed in Cheonggyecheon Stream except when the weather was very dry. \nIn 1923, during the Japanese Colonization Era, drain pipes were constructed in the Cheonggyecheon Stream. After since, excremental water flowed in the stream. In 1924, charged laundry facilities were built at the stream. The Government General of Seoul built a public laundry facility that could be used by 200 people at a time, and received usage fee. Under the Japanese rule, the Cheonggyecheon Stream to women of Joseon was not a resting place, but a place of labor where people paid money to wash clothes.\n": 1,
    "\nHwanghakgyo is a bridge connecting Cheonggyecheon Stream and Hwanghak-dong, a town with a legend that ascetics came in and out riding Hwanghak, a stork. \nAfter the Korean War, the refugees settled around Hwanghak-dong, running street stalls for their livelihood. Hwanghak-dong Market was famous for a market where poor women sold their hair. Their hair were later made into wigs, which was a major export in the 1960s. \nIn 1969, after the construction that covered the Cheonggyecheon Stream around Hwanghak-dong, legally permitted antique stores entered the Hwanghak Market. Hwanghak Market was also called ‘Flea Market’ because of the merchants who ran across the country like fleas to collect antiques. In 1983, a shopping district that traded antiques was created in Janganpyeong, so the antique stores of Hwanghak Market moved to there. \nAs the antique shops moved out, the street stalls began to enter this market. From this time on, Hwanghak-dong Market was called ‘Doggaebi Market’, which means goblin. This name was given because those street vendors quickly disappeared during the crackdown, and then appeared again. It was also because the secondhand goods sold in the Hwanghak-dong Market were reformed like new products through repairs. It was also called ‘Gaemi Market’, which means an ant. This name was created because the street vendors diligently collected old items like ants. Today’s Hwanghakgyo Bridge was newly constructed in 2005.\n\n": 1,
    "\nMalgeunnae Bridge was newly constructed during the restoration project of the Cheonggyecheon Stream in 2005. The bridge was named ‘Malgeunnae’, which is a pure Korean word for Cheonggyecheon Stream.\nWhen Joseon moved its capital to Hanyang, Cheonggyecheon was a natural stream, so it often flooded during summer. Therefore, King Taejong, the third king of Joseon, embarked on a construction to enlarge and deepen the stream. After that, this natural stream was called ‘Gaecheon’. The name ‘Gaecheon’ became ‘Cheonggyecheon’ during the Late Joseon Dynasty. In 1914, the Japanese Government General of Korea investigated the current condition of the stream and arranged every names of rivers. Since then, the term 'Cheonggyecheon' was featured in newspapers.\nThe origin of the name 'Cheonggyecheon' is unknown. It is said that this term is from the Chinese characters that means 'clear and flowing stream', and it is also said that the name came after ‘Cheongpung Gyecheon’, which is a river located at the upper region of the Cheonggyecheon Stream.\n": 1,
    "\nMajeongyo Bridge was initially called ‘Changseonbang Bridge’, and then changed to ‘Taepyeong Bridge’. It gained its current name during the period of King Yeongjo, the 21st king of Joseon, when the Majeon, a market for trading cattle and horse, moved near \nto this bridge. \nThere is Hullyeonwon Park on the south side of Majeongyo Bridge. This is the place where military training and martial arts examination were held during the Joseon Dynasty. Admiral Yi Sun-shin also took a military exam here. There is a famous story that Yi sun-shin was injured at the middle of this test, but he finished his exam after peeling a bark off a willow and wrapping his broken leg with it.\nIn the vicinity of the Majeongyo Bridge, there is Bangsan-dong, where two Gasan, which are artificially built hills with the soil from King Yeongjo’s dredging construction. There were many poor people who lived here in mud huts. They were called the 'snake hunters' since King Yeongjo gave these jobless people the exclusive right to catch and sell snakes. \n\n ": 1,
    "\nNarae Bridge was newly built in front of the Pyeonghwa Market during the restoration work of the Cheonggyecheon Stream in 2005. Pyeonghwa Market was created by the refugees who formed a shantytown around the Cheonggyecheon Stream and sold clothes in street stalls during the Korean War. Most of the refugees were from North Korea, who left their hometowns, and they named this market ‘Pyeonghwa', which means 'peace' in Korean language, with the hopes of peaceful reunification.\nShantytown and street stalls that were concentrated at the Cheonggyecheon Stream burned down by a massive fire in 1958 and 1960, but the Pyeonghwa Market was rebuilt during the covering construction of the stream. Dongdaemun Market, which includes the Pyeonghwa Market, is the center of clothing commerce. The bridge near the market was designed to embody the flutter of butterfly wings with the meaning of rising clothing business in Dongdaemun. \n ": 1,
    "\nThrough the mainstream of Cheonggyecheon to Ogansumun Floodgate, there is an area called ‘Araedae’, which is the downstream. \nIn 1760, King Yeongjo dredged the sand from the Cheonggyecheon Stream’s bottom and planted dozens of willow trees around the stream to prevent the inflow of soil and collapse of river banks. In the 19th Century, the willow trees have overgrown and formed a forest under and over the Ogansumun Floodgate. The view of the yellowish green willow branches shaking by the spring breeze with the Ogansumun Floodgate at the background was splendid. When white willow flowers bloomed in late spring, people even gathered at night to watch the pollen flying like snow.\nWhen the Cheonggyecheon Stream was restored in 2005, willow trees were planted around it.\nIn 1760, the sands that were dug out from the Cheonggyecheon Stream's bottom during the dredging construction were piled up, and they formed two Gasan, which are artificially made hills. The beggars and refugees moved to Gasan Hill and lived together in mud huts. They were called the ‘snake hunters’ because King Yeongjo gave the exclusive right to catch and sell snakes to these jobless people. \n": 1,
    "\nAt the Biudanggyo Bridge to the North Cheonggyecheon Stream, there is a street called Jibongno. It was named after the pen name of Yi Su-gwang, who wrote the first encyclopedia of Korea, Jibong Yuseol. After 40 minutes of walk following the Jibongno Street, there is a little thatched house called ‘Biudang’. This house was the residence of Yu Gwan, the prime minister of King Sejong, the fourth king of Joseon Dynasty. Because Yu Gwan spent his wages for the needy, he was unable to repair his thatched house. When the rain was severe, the rain leaked into the house, so he had to use an umbrella, but he still worried about his neighbours saying, “How could the family without an umbrella avoid this rain?\" After that, people praised the integrity of Yu Gwan and called his house ‘Usangak’, meaning ‘a house that needs an umbrella’.\nYi Hui-geom, Yu Gwan’s descendant, was from a royal family and was a Minister of King Seonjo, the 14th king of Joseon, but he lived at Usangak. One day, a guest visited and said, \"Isn’t this house too shabby for a government official to live?\" Yi Hui-geom answered, “This house is too luxurious compared to an umbrella.”\nYi Hui-geom’s son, Yi Su-gwang called this house ‘Biudang’, meaning ‘a house that would barely shelter the rain.’ Biudanggyo Bridge was built to praise the righteous lives of Yi-Su-gwang’s household.\n": 1,
    "\nMuhakgyo Bridge was newly built during the restoration work of Cheonggyecheon Stream. On the south side of Muhakgyo Bridge, there was Muhak Street, and there was a wide field at this place during Joseon Dynasty. \nGreat Master Muhak came to this wide field under today’s Muhakgyo Bridge to search for Joseon’s future capital. He thought that this place was suitable as the capital. At that moment, an old man driving a cattle passed beside Muhak, shouting, \n“Stupid cow! You are as fool as Muhak.”\nGreat Master Muhak was astonished, so he held the old man and asked, \n“I am that stupid Muhak. I thought this place was suitable as a capital, how do you think?”\nThen the old man answered, \n\"From here, just go four more kilometers.\"\nWhen Muhak went in four more kilometers, there was a place that was suitable as a capital. It was the place that later became Hanyang Fortress. Muhak then met with King Taejo and said that Hanyang was appropriate as the new capital city. The spot where Muhak met with the old man was named ‘Wangsimni’.\n": 1
   },
